# PACKAGES — add, lock, store

File: guide/Packages.oot
User wiki. Canonical URL: https://openooda.org/guide/#Packages.oot

[Home](Home.oot) [Start](Use.oot) [Language](Language.oot) [Commands](Options.oot) [Capabilities](Safety.oot) [How it works](Limits.oot) [Pay](Pay.oot)

## 1. What you install

You add a package with `ooda add hello`.
HTTP 402 on the store is not this page. See [Pay](Pay.oot).
That uses the stock store. You do not type a URL.
You search with `ooda search hello`.
You list stores with `ooda registry`.
You read the lock with `ooda pkg`.

The lock pins a name, a version, a sha256, and a declared cap.

Public examples are `hello`, `mid`, and `world`.
All three declare `FsRead`.
`hello` depends on `mid`.
`mid` depends on `world`.

## 2. The stock store

```
ooda add hello
ooda search hello
ooda registry
ooda registry add https://host
ooda registry remove https://host
```

The stock origin is https://registry.openooda.org
`ooda add` GETs `/catalog` and `/index.minisig`.
It verifies the catalog with `cli/registry.pub`.
If the package manifest lists `pay=` and it is not `free`, add prints that a price is listed. It still downloads. It does not charge.
`ooda search` GETs `/catalog`. For each hit it may GET `name/manifest` and print `pay=` when listed. It does not minisign.
Bare `ooda add NAME` walks every listed store. The first catalog that names the package wins.

An extra store must serve `catalog`, `index.minisig`, and `registry.pub`.
The CLI pins that pubkey on `registry add`.
You cannot remove the stock store.
The human page at the origin searches `/catalog`. It is not a second install path.
The human store at https://registry.openooda.org shows the lock line `name@version#sha256#CapName` and a pay column from each `name/manifest`. Missing pay is free. Browser search is not `ooda add`.

## 3. Add from a file vendor

`ooda add NAME --vendor DIR` reads a folder on disk.
It does not use the network.

```
DIR/
  index
  hello/manifest
  hello/hello.oo
  mid/manifest
  mid/mid.oo
  world/manifest
  world/world.oo
```

Each `manifest` is `key=value`.
It names the payload, the sha256, the caps, and the deps.

```
ooda add hello --vendor ../registry
ooda pkg
```

That resolves `hello` → `mid` → `world`.
It installs each payload into CAS.
It writes the lock.

Local-vendor trust is HMAC with key `ooda-local-pm`.
That HMAC covers the vendor `index`.

## 4. The lock

`ooda pkg` shows the lock.

Each package line has this shape:

```
name@version#sha256#CapName
```

Example after you add `hello`:

```
hello@1.0.0#16c3ceb221e5a2818b340a66796a4a9db0c8a65b5c7bf36aeef285ef160608ce#FsRead
mid@1.0.0#006ee56e6f99f683f3a4398e9f64b2a9eb22356adb0436f711d9444b6abf6c07#FsRead
world@1.0.0#4b23f1b3227a7d15eb42f7e38d46c57fc26ba8619fe8dea1a4d9649e59182703#FsRead
```

The sha256 is the payload pin.
The last field is the declared cap name.

`ooda pkg --json` prints a machine object.

The lock file lives at `.ooda_modules/lock`.
CAS blobs live under `.ooda_modules/cas/<sha256>/`.

## 5. Named capabilities

Lock lines carry a declared cap name.

| Name | Bit |
| :--- | :--- |
| `FsRead` | 1 |
| `Net` | 2 |
| `Process` | 4 |
| `FsWrite` | 8 |

A child package keeps a subset of the parent mask.
Extra caps refuse.

A foreign ELF does not gain Ooda caps because the lock says `FsRead`.
See [Capabilities](Safety.oot).

## 6. Ingest and launch

`ooda pm ingest --from DIR` reads a local recipe.
Ingest pins and wraps.
The wrapper names the payload and the sha256.
The blob stays as it was.

Path traversal, extra caps, and yank refuse.

`ooda launch NAME` re-checks the pin against the live CAS bytes.
`ooda launch NAME --exec` runs the gated payload after sha, caps, and yank are ok.

## 7. The `ooda pm` commands

You can call these as `ooda pm <cmd>`.
Several also work as a top-level `ooda` command.

```
ooda add hello
ooda search hello
ooda registry
ooda pm add
ooda pm ingest
ooda pm launch
ooda pm remove
ooda pm update
ooda pm outdated
ooda pm why
ooda pm tree
ooda pm vendor
ooda pm clean
ooda pm audit
ooda pm sbom
ooda pm pack
```

| Command | What it does |
| :--- | :--- |
| `add` | Install from the stock store. Write the lock. |
| `search` | Search every listed store. |
| `registry` | List stores. `add` / `remove` a URL. |
| `ingest` | Pin and wrap a local recipe. |
| `launch` | Re-check sha, caps, and yank. `--exec NAME` runs the gated payload. |
| `remove` | Drop a name from the lock. |
| `update` | Re-resolve from the vendor. |
| `outdated` | Compare lock versions to the catalog. |
| `why` | Show who depends on a name. |
| `tree` | Print the dependency tree. |
| `vendor` | List the file vendor. |
| `clean` | Drop unused CAS dirs. |
| `audit` | Report yank, advisory, and pin hits. |
| `sbom` | Print a CycloneDX-lite object from the lock. |
| `pack` | Write a file-backed vendor tree from the lock. |

`ooda add` is the same path as `ooda pm add`.
`ooda pkg` is the inventory command.

## 8. Two trust paths

1. Stock and extra HTTPS stores. minisign of `/catalog`. Stock uses `cli/registry.pub`. An extra store pins its own `registry.pub`.
2. Local vendor. HMAC key `ooda-local-pm` on the vendor `index`.

Fetch does not replace the stock pubkey.

